<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurymedon River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Köprüçay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eurymedon, now the Köprüçay, is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> that flows from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> mountains to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> through ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>. The city of Aspendos stood near its lower course, and Selge lay above its gorge. Around 466 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> near its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurymedon-river.jpg" alt="Eurymedon River" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralf Rochow · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurymedon_Bridge,_Selge,_Turkey._Pic_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
